Let $g$ be a <p-regular element>, with <eigenvalues> $\lambda_i$ on $V$. On both $g^{-1}$ acting on $V$ and $g$ acting on the <dual representation> $V^*$, the <eigenvalues> are $\lambda_i^{-1}$. Multiplicative lifts of <roots of unity> satisfy $\widehat{\lambda_i^{-1}}=\widehat\lambda_i^{-1}=\overline{\widehat\lambda_i}$. Hence
$$
\boxed{\chi_V(g^{-1})=\overline{\chi_V(g)}=\chi_{V^*}(g)}.
$$
All these identities concern the domain of a <Brauer character>, namely the <p-regular elements>. If necessary, extend the <p-modular system> to contain the relevant <roots of unity> and use one consistent choice of lifts.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

For a <p-regular element> $g$, diagonalize its actions on $V$ and $V'$. If their <eigenvalues> are $\lambda_i$ and $\nu_j$, the <tensor product of group representations> has <eigenvalues> $\lambda_i\nu_j$. The lift of <roots of unity> is multiplicative, so
$$
\chi_{V\otimes V'}(g)=\sum_{i,j}\widehat\lambda_i\widehat\nu_j=\chi_V(g)\chi_{V'}(g).
$$
Thus $\boxed{\chi_{V\otimes V'}=\chi_V\chi_{V'}}$ as <Brauer characters>.

= iii
{parent=b}
